/-
Papers/SIG/KochenSpecker.lean

The Kochen–Specker theorem, discharging the named hypothesis `KochenSpecker H`
of SIG 46 (main.tex:1257–1277): for a complex Hilbert space `H` with
`dim H > 2` (any dimension, finite or not) there is no unital σ-additive map
`P(H) → {0,1}`; so SIG 46's Kochen–Specker clause holds outright
(`sig46_unconditional`).

Plan.
1. Combinatorics (`ks_noColouring`): the Conway–Kochen set of 31 integer
   rays in `ℝ³` (coordinates in `{0,±1,±2}`; found by computer search as a
   minimal uncolourable subset of all such rays) admits no `{0,1}`-colouring
   with no two orthogonal rays coloured `1` and some ray of each of its 17
   orthogonal triples coloured `1`.  Proof: a DPLL tree with 6 leaves (split
   on rays 0, 3, 2/14), each closed by an explicit unit-propagation chain
   (generated by computer search, checked by Lean).
2. Frames.  For an orthonormal family `f : J × Fin 3 → H` (a "copy" of
   `ℂ³ ⊗ ℓ²(J)` inside `H`) and an integer ray `r`, put
   `g_r(j) = Σ_k r_k f(j,k)`, `K_r = (span g_r)ᗮᗮ` (closed), `P_r` its
   projection, and `R` the projection onto `W = (span f)ᗮ`.  Orthogonal rays
   give orthogonal `K_r` (so `P_r + P_s ≤ 1`); an orthogonal triple `a,b,c`
   gives `P_a + P_b + P_c + R = 1`: the defect `y` is orthogonal to every
   `g_a(j), g_b(j), g_c(j)`, hence (the triple spans `ℚ³`: an explicit
   integer identity, checked by `decide`) to every `f(j,k)`, so `y ∈ W ∩ Wᗮ`.
3. Valuations.  For a σ-additive `ω : P(H) → {0,1}` with `ω 1 = 1`:
   orthogonal projections are never both `1`, and of four projections summing
   to `1` one is `1`.  So if `ω R = 0` the colouring `i ↦ ω(P_{ray i})`
   contradicts step 1: every frame has `ω R = 1` (`frame_complement_true`).
4. Two frames with orthogonal complements `R₁ ⊥ R₂` then contradict step 3.
   Take a Hilbert basis `(b_i)_{i∈ι}`.  If `ι` is infinite, `ι ≃ ι × Fin 3`
   and one frame is the whole basis (`R = 0`).  If `ι` is finite,
   `|ι| = n = 3m + k ≥ 3` (`n = dim H`), and the frames are the first `3m`
   and the last `3m` basis vectors; their complements are spanned by the
   last `k` and the first `k` basis vectors, orthogonal as `k ≤ 3m`
   (Parseval).
Only finite additivity of `ω` is used.
-/
import Papers.SIG.Examples

